<sheets>
<sheet>
<plain>
<text x="22.86" y="231.14" size="1.778" layer="97">DCE Mode</text>
<text x="104.14" y="231.14" size="1.778" layer="97">DTE Mode</text>
<text x="198.12" y="88.9" size="1.016" layer="98" rot="R180">Protect against RS232
voltage</text>
<text x="228.6" y="40.64" size="1.016" layer="98" rot="R180">Pins 6 and 20 tied together to simplify our design</text>
<text x="137.16" y="48.26" size="1.27" layer="98">If RS232 voltage is active,
AVR should be disconnected</text>
<text x="220.98" y="238.76" size="1.778" layer="98">For 20mA LEDs, use 1k resistor</text>
<text x="74.93" y="109.22" size="1.016" layer="98">Leave SS unconnected</text>
<text x="185.42" y="38.1" size="1.016" layer="98">Pin 25 disconnected for safety</text>
<text x="203.2" y="177.8" size="1.016" layer="98">Raspberry Pi 3.3V interface</text>
<text x="-2.54" y="251.46" size="1.778" layer="98">Warren suggested trying 1k for these pull-downs, as 10k is too strong</text>
<text x="154.94" y="177.8" size="1.778" layer="98">external pull-up because
it is shared by two AVRs</text>
<text x="101.6" y="167.64" size="1.778" layer="98">Protects VP931 hardware
from serial I/O on USART1
and makes sure PR8210A output
does not conflict with PR8210
output</text>
<text x="2.54" y="-17.78" size="1.778" layer="98">IR interface for cliff hanger, g2g, etc</text>
<text x="2.286" y="141.224" size="1.778" layer="98">Cuttable trace so we can optionally use 3.5mm stereo plug and RCA video jack</text>
</plain>
